Part of Cloudberry's pax_schedule on the port's cluster: the differences
from Cloudberry's expected output that have been reviewed, and are the
port's for a reason of its own.

Each file is one test's difference in the form ../../singlenode/canon.pl
gives it, named for the expected output it differs from, a test in a
directory with its / as _ (statistics_min_max_time_types.diff).  run.sh
counts a test as passing when gpdiff.pl, under Cloudberry's init_file,
PAX's and the port's, finds no difference or exactly the file's.

alter_distributed  ALTER TABLE ... SET DISTRIBUTED BY, which Cloudberry
  carries out by writing the rows into a new table and swapping the two
  tables' files, and PAX's fast sequences with them; the port by copying
  the rows out to a temporary table, emptying the table and putting them
  back (gp_sql's distribution.c).  There is no swap, so the fault the test
  injects into it, pax_finish_swap_fast_fastsequence, never fires: the
  ALTER commits where Cloudberry's is rolled back by the segments' panic,
  and the fast sequences read after it are the ones the redistributed rows
  gave the table, as after the test's first ALTER, where both agree.

ddl  psql's \d+ of a table with NOT NULL columns, which PostgreSQL 19's
  psql lists under "Not-null constraints:", as PostgreSQL 18 keeps them in
  pg_constraint.  And gp_toolkit.gp_size_of_schema_disk: the port's
  gp_toolkit has gp_ao's functions alone, and not yet Cloudberry's
  gp_toolkit extension, whose view it is.

types  a pg_lsn, which PostgreSQL 19 prints with the low half in eight
  digits, 16/00000000, where Cloudberry's printed 16/0.

filter_tree  PAX's LOG of the filter it makes of a scan's conditions, on a
  segment.  Cloudberry sends a segment its plan whole; the port's planner
  sends a scan's conditions only where they mean the same on a segment
  (gp_core's gp_scan.c), so none that calls intrc(), the test's volatile
  function: the coordinator evaluates it on the rows the segments send, so
  an OR with it sends no condition and an AND the others alone.  And a
  join whose condition on one table's column Cloudberry's planner copies
  to the column of the other that it is joined to
  (gp_enable_predicate_propagation, generate_implied_quals() in its
  pathkeys.c) and PostgreSQL 19's planner does not: t1's scan is sent
  v1 > 1 without v1 < 10, which would have skipped its second file.  The
  counts are the same.

statistics/min_max_time_types, statistics/min_max_other_types  the same
  LOG, of the files and groups PAX's statistics skip, for conditions whose
  functions are stable: a date or a timestamp compared with a timestamptz
  and the other way round, and a time with a timetz and the other way
  round, whose answers depend on TimeZone, and a money constant
  cast from an integer, which depends on lc_monetary.  The coordinator
  evaluates them, and no file or group is skipped on a segment.  The
  counts are the same.
